<!--
  ats_deprecated_retention_fields.xml — the withheld-retention "2" family is
  present and the document is still ACCEPTED.

  Rule exercised: detalleComprasType keeps estabRetencion2 (ats.xsd:794),
  ptoEmiRetencion2 (ats.xsd:801), secRetencion2 (ats.xsd:809),
  autRetencion2 (ats.xsd:816) and fechaEmiRet2 (ats.xsd:824). Each one is
  annotated "eliminado se mantiene por compatibilidad" — removed from the norm,
  retained for compatibility. All five are minOccurs="0", so emitting them is
  legal and so is omitting them.

  THIS IS WHERE THE SCHEMA IS NOT THE NORM, in the permissive direction: the
  schema still accepts a family the SRI retired. This fixture exists to pin
  that acceptance, so that nobody "corrects" ats.xsd by deleting the family.
  Dropping the elements from an official published artifact would change its
  bytes and break the sha256 test that proves the shipped copy is the SRI's;
  changing the schema to make a test pass is exactly the failure mode that pin
  prevents. The normative answer — emit nothing — lives in the §5.4 trap table,
  not in the XSD.

  The family is placed exactly where the sequence declares it, between
  valRetServ100 (ats.xsd:755) and docModificado (ats.xsd:832), with the
  "1" family omitted; skipping optional particles is legal, reordering them is
  not.
-->
